  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe vort max from yesterday's MCV over AZ will continue to track\naround the upper high. It looks like it may merge with a weak \nshortwave tonight over UT and continue to round the high on \nThursday, sparking thunderstorm activity across eastern CO on \nThursday afternoon. Regardless of the cause of the thunderstorms,\nmodels have been consistent that an outflow boundary will push \nsouthward across northeast and east central NM Thursday night into\nFriday. By Friday afternoon, easterly upslope flow will allow \nstorms to develop along the Central Mountain Chain. Meanwhile, the\nupper high will have shifted westward over northern AZ. This will\nallow storms to shift southeastward across the plains Friday \nafternoon and evening. Heavy rainfall and gusty winds will be the \nprimary threats. Additional storms should also develop across the \nSouthwest Mountains on Friday, but drier air moving into northwest\nNM around the high should limit thunderstorm coverage there. The \nhigh remains over AZ on Saturday. Storms should still develop over\nthe Central Mtn Chain and Southwest Mountains on Saturday, but \nstorm motions will be more due north to south, which will keep \nstorms near or over the mountains through the afternoon and \nevening. Though the Ruidoso area could see rain on Friday, \nSaturday looks to be the next day of significant concern for burn \nscar flash flooding in the Ruidoso area. \n\nA downtrend in thunderstorm coverage remains forecast for Sunday\nas much drier air will filter in from the north around the\nweakening upper high. PWATs will finally dip below one inch. Storm\ncoverage may trend up slightly for Monday, but PWATs will still be\ntrending downward for most areas, perhaps as low as 0.75\" at ABQ in\nthe aftn. A couple of strong outflow boundaries moving into NM \nfrom both CO and AZ on Tuesday should help increase moisture and \nthunderstorm chances.",
